---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ImageSizeEstimator from '../../components/ImageSizeEstimator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Docker Image Size Estimator" description="Estimate final Docker image size from your base image choice, app code, and dependencies.">
  <ImageSizeEstimator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why base image choice dominates final size</h2>
  <p>Your application code and dependencies are often small compared to the base OS image — choosing alpine over a full Debian/Ubuntu base, or a slim variant over a full language runtime image, can cut hundreds of megabytes before you've added a single line of your own code.</p>

  <h2>How to use this estimator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Select a base image.</li>
    <li>Enter your estimated app code and dependency sizes.</li>
    <li>See the projected total image size.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using a full base image "just to be safe"</strong> when a slim or alpine variant would work fine for most applications, needlessly bloating image size and pull time.</li>
    <li><strong>Not using multi-stage builds</strong> for compiled languages, shipping build tools and intermediate artifacts that aren't needed at runtime.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting layer caching</strong> — reordering Dockerfile instructions so rarely-changing layers (dependencies) come before frequently-changing ones (source code) speeds up rebuilds significantly.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Comparing base image options before finalizing a Dockerfile</li>
    <li>Estimating registry storage and pull time costs for a new image</li>
    <li>Justifying a migration from a full base image to a slimmer alternative</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Is a smaller image always better?</strong> Generally yes for pull speed and attack surface, but alpine's musl libc can occasionally cause compatibility issues with certain native dependencies — test thoroughly before switching.</p>
</ToolLayout>
